<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashidun Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rashidun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rashidun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> was the first Islamic caliphate, ruled by the four rightly guided caliphs Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali from the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> in 632 CE until 661 CE. It reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and the Byzantine provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Civil war after Uthman's assassination ended it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> succeeded it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate.jpg" alt="Rashidun Caliphate" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad adil at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_adil-Rashidun-empire-at-its-peak-close.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the traditional account, Muhammad began receiving what Muslims consider divine revelations in 610 CE and migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622 CE. By his death in 632 CE almost all the tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but Muslim leaders disagreed over who should lead the community. The Rashidun caliphs were not hereditary rulers: they were chosen by a small group of prominent members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> tribe in shūrā, or consultation, or nominated by their predecessor, as Umar was by Abu Bakr.</p><p>Abu Bakr, Muhammad's close companion and father-in-law, was chosen first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and reigned from 632 to 634. Umar ruled from 634 to 644, Uthman from 644 to 656, and Ali, Muhammad's cousin and son-in-law, from 656 to 661. The term Rashidun, meaning rightly guided, comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> doctrine. Shi'a Muslims accept only Ali as a legitimate caliph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After suppressing rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, Abu Bakr sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> into Sasanian-controlled Iraq in 633 with an army of 18,000, and Khalid won four battles in succession before the city of Hira fell. Under Umar the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> expanded at an unprecedented rate and took more than two-thirds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and nearly the whole of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>; Uthman's reign completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 651. The conquests are explained partly by the exhaustion of both empires in the Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602 to 628.</p><p>Umar allowed local administrations of conquered lands to continue much as before, appointing a governor with full powers over each province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">Dhimmis</a>, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> and Christians, kept their religion and a measure of autonomy in return for paying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>. Uthman was assassinated in June 656 and Ali's accession led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, in which the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, refused to recognise him until Uthman's killers were punished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-siffin">Battle of Siffin</a> between Ali's Iraqi army and Mu'awiya's Syrian forces ended in stalemate and an inconclusive arbitration, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> seceded. A Kharijite assassinated Ali in 661. Mu'awiya then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, obliged Ali's son Hasan to abdicate and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. Three of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> died by assassination.</p><p>Later historians wrote of this period as a golden age, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> tradition holds it second in sanctity only to Muhammad's lifetime. Some scholars, among them Donner and Chamieh, write that the community came to regard the four as rightly guided only after memories of the fighting had faded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rashidun Caliphate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In late 639 Amr ibn al-As invaded for the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>, defeated the Roman army at Heliopolis in 640, and took the fortress of Babylon in 641.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire was therefore without effective central authority just as the armies of the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b> invaded in 633 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Umayyad Caliphate was established in 661 CE after the assassination of Ali ibn Abi Talib, the fourth of the <b>Rashidun</b> caliphs and the cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Christian era the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b> invaded Makuria in 651 and was repulsed twice, and the Baqt treaty that followed prevented further Arab invasions in exchange for 360 slaves a year.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Ali died of his wounds two days later, which ended the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His death in 651, after successive defeats by the <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b>, marked the end of the last pre-Islamic Iranian empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
